WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.168
Instructor: This video is just additional practice

00:02.168 --> 00:05.460
or reinforcement on the basic pushing workflow,

00:05.460 --> 00:09.510
add, commit, push up to GitHub and repeats.

00:09.510 --> 00:10.830
So if you feel good about that,

00:10.830 --> 00:12.450
move on to the next video where I talk

00:12.450 --> 00:15.420
about cloning your own repos, option two.

00:15.420 --> 00:19.590
But for now, I'm gonna go back to my first novel

00:19.590 --> 00:21.720
and nothing super exciting in here.

00:21.720 --> 00:24.390
But why don't we make a new branch?

00:24.390 --> 00:26.700
What branch are we on right now?

00:26.700 --> 00:28.920
We're on this empty branch.

00:28.920 --> 00:30.930
What would be a good branch to make?

00:30.930 --> 00:32.520
Why don't we go back to master?

00:32.520 --> 00:36.120
Okay, so I'm gonna switch, git switch master,

00:36.120 --> 00:38.490
and I'm gonna pretend to do some more work.

00:38.490 --> 00:40.920
We've got chapter 2, 3, 4.

00:40.920 --> 00:43.110
Why don't I remove this mood board?

00:43.110 --> 00:45.000
I'm just gonna get rid of that.

00:45.000 --> 00:49.332
So I'm gonna do an RM dash RF mood board.

00:49.332 --> 00:51.180
So I got rid of that folder.

00:51.180 --> 00:53.970
I'll do a git status, I'll add all those changes,

00:53.970 --> 00:58.970
git commits, delete mood board folder.

01:00.330 --> 01:02.670
So now we have this new commit

01:02.670 --> 01:05.880
that only exists here locally on my repo,

01:05.880 --> 01:10.320
on my laptop on the master branch, not up on GitHub.

01:10.320 --> 01:12.168
So now I'm gonna switch to the empty branch,

01:12.168 --> 01:14.550
git switch empty.

01:14.550 --> 01:16.860
I'm gonna make another commit on this branch.

01:16.860 --> 01:18.545
So what do we have in here?

01:18.545 --> 01:20.250
Not a whole lot.

01:20.250 --> 01:22.110
Why don't I make a new file?

01:22.110 --> 01:24.960
We'll call this one characters, outline,

01:24.960 --> 01:27.325
how about Summary dot txt?

01:27.325 --> 01:31.050
And then I'm just gonna add that empty file and commit it.

01:31.050 --> 01:32.703
Add summary file.

01:33.540 --> 01:36.323
Okay, so now we have a commit here on empty

01:36.323 --> 01:38.280
that is not on GitHub,

01:38.280 --> 01:40.926
and we have a commit on master that is not on GitHub.

01:40.926 --> 01:43.569
So let's push both of them up.

01:43.569 --> 01:45.960
It's gonna take two commands, right?

01:45.960 --> 01:48.810
We need to first decide, let's push up master branch

01:48.810 --> 01:50.700
or let's push up the empty branch.

01:50.700 --> 01:52.320
Remember the syntax, git push,

01:52.320 --> 01:54.000
and then our remote is origin.

01:54.000 --> 01:55.410
That's the only one we have.

01:55.410 --> 01:58.379
And then I'll push the master branch to begin with.

01:58.379 --> 02:03.368
Okay, so if I come back to my GitHub and I refresh,

02:03.368 --> 02:06.150
I don't see anything new here, right?

02:06.150 --> 02:09.030
Because I'm still on the empty branch on GitHub.

02:09.030 --> 02:10.620
I'm looking at the empty branch

02:10.620 --> 02:13.284
but if I switch to the master branch,

02:13.284 --> 02:16.127
we can see this new commit two minutes ago,

02:16.127 --> 02:19.170
delete mood board folder.

02:19.170 --> 02:22.500
And we don't see the mood board here anymore at all.

02:22.500 --> 02:24.626
So I can take a look at that commit,

02:24.626 --> 02:27.060
specifically see what was deleted,

02:27.060 --> 02:29.610
as you see there the whole mood board directory

02:29.610 --> 02:31.080
and its image contents.

02:31.080 --> 02:32.790
So that new commit was pushed up

02:32.790 --> 02:35.340
whatever new commits existed on the master branch

02:35.340 --> 02:38.910
but on the empty branch, nothing has changed on GitHub.

02:38.910 --> 02:42.350
Remember on my local empty branch, which I'm on right now,

02:42.350 --> 02:44.070
we have this new file summary

02:44.070 --> 02:46.770
and we have a commit called, well what was it?

02:46.770 --> 02:47.730
Add summary file.

02:47.730 --> 02:48.900
That was the message.

02:48.900 --> 02:50.250
So if I wanna push that up,

02:50.250 --> 02:53.943
I need to do git push origin and then the empty branch.

02:55.830 --> 02:58.314
And if we head over here,

02:58.314 --> 03:01.830
refresh at some points, here we are,

03:01.830 --> 03:04.260
empty had recent pushes less than a minute ago

03:04.260 --> 03:07.500
and we see summary dot txt, that was pushed up

03:07.500 --> 03:09.870
and the commit that it was associated with,

03:09.870 --> 03:12.930
add summary file, two days ago.

03:12.930 --> 03:14.160
So that's, yeah,

03:14.160 --> 03:16.530
that's pretty much all I wanted to show you here, right?

03:16.530 --> 03:19.860
That basic workflow, do some stuff locally on a branch,

03:19.860 --> 03:22.530
make some commits, and then you push that branch up

03:22.530 --> 03:24.030
and that about wraps up the basics

03:24.030 --> 03:25.710
of using the push command, right?

03:25.710 --> 03:27.360
We have a branch locally,

03:27.360 --> 03:30.540
we push it up with git push remote and branch name.

03:30.540 --> 03:33.870
Then GitHub now has that branch hosted in the Cloud.

03:33.870 --> 03:35.550
And then we repeat that

03:35.550 --> 03:37.470
if we want to push another branch up.

03:37.470 --> 03:39.911
Now you don't have to push every branch you make up.

03:39.911 --> 03:43.980
Often what you'll do is have a master or a main branch

03:43.980 --> 03:46.740
and then you'll have branches where you work on features.

03:46.740 --> 03:49.350
You can merge those into master slash main

03:49.350 --> 03:52.676
and then push that new master slash main work up.

03:52.676 --> 03:54.900
Or you can push branches up to GitHub

03:54.900 --> 03:56.610
and just have a whole bunch of branches.

03:56.610 --> 03:57.570
But that's something we'll talk about

03:57.570 --> 03:58.630
when we get to workflows.

03:58.630 --> 03:59.733
All right.
